    "payload": {
      "content": "When reviewing the significance of self-supervised learning (SSL) papers, prioritize the theoretical contribution's ability to reconcile distinct method families (e.g., asymmetric architectures vs. explicit regularization) as a strength for broad research impact (SIGN-BRD). For domain impact (SIGN-DOM), value practical simplifications such as removing EMA target networks that reduce computational overhead. However, critically assess state-of-the-art improvements (SIGN-SOT) by checking if empirical validation is limited to small-scale datasets (e.g., CIFAR-10) and small backbones (e.g., VGG11); such limitations often weaken claims of substantial SOTA improvement. Note that the absence of societal impact discussions (SIGN-IMP) is common in theoretical SSL work but should be acknowledged.",
